<h1>Conveyor Frame, Drive &amp; Pulley Design</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Specifying a conveyor means choosing the frame, conveying surface, drive, pulleys, take-up, bearings, and sensors so they work as one system. These choices are linked: the belt sets the minimum pulley diameter and the take-up style, the drive position sets where tension is highest and how tight the end transfers can be, and the frame material sets what bearings and fasteners can be used. This design guide covers how to specify each part and how the choices interact. For a map of all <a href="/components/">conveyor components</a>, including guide rails, cleaning systems, and wear parts, see the components hub.</p>
<h2 id="conveyor-anatomy">Parts of a Belt Conveyor</h2>
<p>A belt conveyor has a frame (side channels) carrying a slider bed or carrying rollers, an endless belt looped around a head (drive) pulley and a tail pulley, return rollers under the frame, a take-up that sets belt tension, and a gearmotor that turns the head pulley. Bearings hold every pulley shaft, legs set the height, and guide rails keep product on the belt. The diagram shows a common end-drive layout in side view.</p>
