<h2 id="interact-heading">How Component Choices Interact</h2>
<p>Most specification errors come from choosing one part in isolation. Use this table to check what each decision constrains before you finalize it.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Decision</th>
<th>What It Constrains</th>
<th>What to Confirm</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Belt or roller type</td>
<td>Minimum pulley or nose roller diameter, drive method (friction pulley or sprocket), take-up style, bed type (slider bed or roller bed)</td>
<td>Belt manufacturer's minimum pulley diameter and rated tension</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Drive position</td>
<td>Where belt tension peaks, nose size at each end, transfer gap to the next conveyor, reversing capability, motor access</td>
<td>Transfer gap the smallest product can bridge at each end</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conveyor length and load</td>
<td>Belt pull, motor and gearbox size, take-up travel, number of frame supports</td>
<td>Belt pull calculation from the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Frame material</td>
<td>Bearing housing material, fasteners, guide rail brackets, sensor mounts, weld finish</td>
<td>Every part touching the frame is rated for the same washdown and chemicals</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Environment (washdown, temperature)</td>
<td>Belt material, bearing seals and lubricant, motor enclosure, sensor housing rating</td>
<td>Rated temperature and ingress protection of each component, not just the frame</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Controls approach</td>
<td>Sensor count and placement, encoder need, zone length on accumulation conveyors</td>
<td>Sensor positions agreed before frame drilling and guarding layout</td>
</tr>
