<h2 id="frames-heading">Conveyor Frames</h2>
<p>The frame is the structural backbone of every conveyor. It supports the belt or rollers, the drive system, the product load, and any mounted accessories (guide rails, sensors, guards). Frame material, cross-section profile, and joint construction determine load capacity, corrosion resistance, cleanability, and cost.</p>
<h3>Carbon Steel</h3>
<p>Carbon steel frames are the lowest-cost option for dry, non-corrosive environments. Frames are fabricated from structural channel, angle, or tube steel, then painted or powder-coated for corrosion protection. Carbon steel handles heavy loads. Rated capacity depends on channel size, span between supports, and the allowed deflection, so confirm the builder's rated load per foot for your support spacing. Most standard-duty conveyor product lines use carbon steel as the default frame material. Paint or powder coating must be maintained; chips and scratches expose bare steel to rust.</p>
<h3>Stainless Steel</h3>
<p>Stainless steel frames are required for washdown, food-grade, pharmaceutical, and corrosive environments. Type 304 stainless steel handles most food and beverage applications, including daily washdown with standard cleaning chemicals. Type 316 stainless steel resists chlorides, acids, and aggressive sanitizers, required for salt, citric acid, and chlorinated-cleaning environments. Stainless frames use TIG-welded construction with ground and polished welds to eliminate crevices that harbor bacteria. Stainless steel conveyor systems for sanitary applications are widely available from packaging conveyor specialists.</p>
<h3>Aluminum Extrusion</h3>
<p>Aluminum extrusion frames use T-slot profiles that bolt together without welding. This modular construction allows quick assembly, reconfiguration, and disassembly. Aluminum frames are much lighter than steel frames of similar size, naturally corrosion-resistant, and offer a clean appearance suitable for cleanroom, pharmaceutical, and light-duty packaging applications. Aluminum extrusion-framed conveyors are available from multiple conveyor builders. Load capacity is generally lower than a comparable steel frame and depends on profile size and span, so confirm the rated load per foot for the chosen profile and support spacing.</p>
<h3>Painted and Powder-Coated Steel</h3>
<p>Painted carbon steel frames add a layer of corrosion protection at lower cost than stainless steel. Powder coating is more durable than wet paint and resists chipping, scratching, and chemical exposure better. Standard colors are machine gray, safety blue, or custom RAL colors. Powder-coated frames suit dry food packaging (packaged products, not exposed food) and general industrial environments. For exposed food and full washdown, stainless steel is the better choice. Replacement <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">conveyor parts</a> and accessories must match the frame material for corrosion compatibility.</p>
<h2 id="belts-heading">Belt Types</h2>
<p>The belt is the product contact surface. Belt material determines grip, temperature tolerance, chemical resistance, FDA compliance, and wear life. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">conveyor belting guide</a> covers belt materials in depth. This section summarizes the five primary belt types used in packaging conveyor systems.</p>
<h3>PVC Belts</h3>
<p>PVC belts are the lowest-cost option for dry, room-temperature conveying of packaged goods, cartons, and cases. Available in smooth, rough-top, and fabric-textured surfaces. Operating temperature limits vary by compound, so check the belt manufacturer's rating. Most general-purpose PVC formulations are not intended for direct food contact. PVC belting is available from all major belt suppliers in a wide range of surface textures and thicknesses.</p>
<h3>Polyurethane (PU) Belts</h3>
<p>Polyurethane is a common food-grade belt material. Food-grade PU formulations are made to comply with FDA food-contact regulations, so ask the supplier for the specific 21 CFR citation and a compliance letter. PU belts resist abrasion, cuts, oils, fats, and cleaning chemicals. Temperature limits vary by formulation. PU belts cost more than PVC but generally offer better hygiene and chemical resistance. They are the default belt for sanitary conveyor applications.</p>
<h3>Modular Plastic Belts</h3>
<p>Modular plastic belts consist of interlocking modules connected by hinge rods. Damaged modules are individually replaceable without removing the entire belt, which shortens repair downtime. Available in acetal, polypropylene, polyethylene, and nylon, with FDA-compliant formulations offered. Resin sets the temperature range: <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-800/flat-top" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox rates its Series 800 Flat Top</a> at 34 to 220°F in polypropylene and -50 to 150°F in polyethylene. Modular belts handle curves, inclines, and heavy loads. Higher upfront cost can be offset by lower maintenance cost over the belt's service life.</p>
<h3>Wire Mesh and Metal Belts</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts handle high-temperature applications (ovens, dryers, fryers) and heavy loads. Stainless steel wire mesh handles temperatures well beyond polymer belt limits, with the exact rating set by alloy and construction. Flat wire belts provide a smooth surface for small products. Chain-edge belts handle heavy loads in accumulation applications. Metal belts are common in baking, snack food, and heat-treat applications where high temperatures and heavy loads exceed the capabilities of polymer belts.</p>
<h3>Steel Belts</h3>
<p>Solid stainless steel belts (also called steel bands) provide a flat, non-porous surface for cooling, freezing, and processing applications. Steel belts are used in chocolate cooling, pharmaceutical tablet pressing, and chemical processing where absolute surface cleanliness and temperature control are required.</p>
<h2 id="rollers-heading">Conveyor Rollers</h2>
<p>Rollers are the conveying surface on roller conveyors. Roller diameter, material, bearing type, and spacing determine load capacity, speed, noise, and service life. For a detailed overview of roller conveyor types, see the <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors page</a>.</p>
<h3>Gravity Rollers</h3>
<p>Gravity rollers are unpowered. Product moves by gravity (on a slight decline) or by manual push. Gravity rollers are the simplest and lowest-cost conveyor component. Standard gravity rollers use carbon steel tubes with precision bearings. Roller diameter and tube wall thickness scale with product weight. Roller spacing is determined by product size. <a href="https://www.ashlandconveyor.com/pages/roller-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ashland's roller conveyor guide</a> says a minimum of three rollers should be in contact with the load at all times, so the shortest product sets the maximum roller center. Gravity roller conveyors are one of the most widely available conveyor products, offered by nearly every conveyor manufacturer.</p>
<h3>Powered Rollers</h3>
<p>Powered rollers are driven by belts, chains, or line shafts from an external motor. Belt-driven live roller (BDLR) conveyors use a flat belt running under the rollers. The belt contacts the bottom of each roller, spinning it. Chain-driven live roller (CDLR) conveyors use roller chain and sprockets to drive each roller, handling heavier loads than belt-driven rollers. Line-shaft driven conveyors use a rotating shaft with O-ring or spool drives to power individual rollers.</p>
<h3>Accumulation Rollers</h3>
<p>Accumulation rollers allow products to queue on the conveyor without back pressure. Minimum-pressure accumulation conveyors disengage roller drive when a zone is full, the drive belt or chain slips on a clutch mechanism. Zero-pressure accumulation conveyors use independently controlled zones with photoeye sensors, each zone stops its rollers when the downstream zone is occupied. MDR (motorized drive roller) conveyors achieve zero-pressure accumulation using a low-voltage DC motor embedded inside a drive roller in each zone. MDR rollers are available from several global suppliers specializing in motorized roller technology.</p>
<h2 id="drives-heading">Drive Configurations</h2>
<p>The drive system converts electrical power into belt or roller motion. Drive configuration, end drive, center drive, or head drive, affects belt tension distribution, transfer point design, and maintenance access. For motor and VFD selection details, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">motors and drives guide</a>.</p>
<h3>End Drive</h3>
<p>End drives place the motor and drive pulley at the discharge end of the conveyor. The motor mounts below or beside the conveyor frame and drives the head pulley via a gear reducer. This is the most common belt conveyor drive configuration. The top belt strand runs in tension from the tail pulley to the drive pulley. End drives are simple, reliable, and easy to maintain. One limitation: the drive pulley at the discharge end creates a larger nose radius, making close-gap transfers more difficult.</p>
<h3>Center Drive</h3>
<p>Center drives place the motor underneath the conveyor, typically at the midpoint. The belt wraps around a drive pulley in a module on the return side, with snub rollers setting the wrap angle. Many center drives can run in either direction. Both ends of the conveyor can have small-diameter nose rollers (knife-edge) for tight product transfers. Center drives are common on food-grade conveyors where sanitary design requires small-diameter nose rollers at both ends.</p>
<h3>Head Drive</h3>
<p>Head drive is functionally similar to an end drive but specifically refers to roller conveyors where the motor drives the head (discharge) roller directly. The head roller transmits power to adjacent rollers via belts, chains, or O-rings. Head drives are standard on BDLR and CDLR conveyors. The motor mounts at the conveyor end, making it accessible for maintenance.</p>
<h2 id="pulleys-heading">Pulleys &amp; Bearings</h2>
<p>Pulleys redirect and tension the conveyor belt. Every belt conveyor has at least two pulleys, a drive pulley and a tail pulley. Larger systems add snub pulleys to increase belt wrap angle on the drive pulley, and take-up pulleys to maintain proper belt tension.</p>
<h3>Drive Pulley</h3>
<p>The drive pulley transmits motor torque to the belt. Larger diameter drive pulleys provide more belt wrap angle and greater traction. Drive pulleys are crowned (slightly larger diameter at center) to track the belt. Lagging, a rubber or ceramic coating bonded to the pulley surface, increases the coefficient of friction between pulley and belt. Lagging is essential for high-tension, incline, and wet environments where belt slip is a risk.</p>
<h3>Tail Pulley</h3>
<p>The tail pulley is located at the infeed end of the conveyor. It redirects the belt from the return strand to the carrying strand. Tail pulleys are typically the same diameter as or smaller than the drive pulley. Wing-style tail pulleys have vanes that shed debris, preventing material buildup that causes belt mistracking.</p>
<h3>Take-Up Mechanisms</h3>
<p>Take-up mechanisms set and maintain belt tension. Fabric belt makers specify tension as a percentage of initial elongation, and <a href="https://www.habasit.com/-/media/Project/Habasit/NewWebsite/Downloads/Products/General-conveyor-belts/Fabric-Conveyor-Belts-Installation-and-Maintenance-Guide.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Habasit's installation guide</a> says the tension pulley must stay exactly at right angles to the belt while tensioning, so the take-up must have enough travel and move both ends evenly. Screw take-ups use threaded rods to adjust the tail pulley position manually, simple and low-cost for short conveyors. Gravity take-ups use a weighted pulley that automatically maintains constant belt tension, preferred for long conveyors and systems where belt elongation is significant. Spring-loaded take-ups provide automatic tension adjustment for medium-length conveyors.</p>
<h3>Bearings</h3>
<p>Bearings support pulley shafts and roller shafts. Pillow block bearings and flanged bearings are standard on conveyor pulleys. Sealed bearings prevent contamination from dust, water, and cleaning chemicals. Stainless steel bearings and food-grade bearings with H1-rated lubricant are required for washdown and food-contact environments. Bearing failure is one of the top causes of conveyor downtime. Predictive maintenance using vibration monitoring and temperature monitoring detects bearing degradation before failure. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/">design guide hub</a> covers maintenance planning as part of the design process.</p>
<h2 id="sensors-heading">Sensors &amp; Detection</h2>
<p>Sensors provide the data that controls use to start, stop, speed-match, accumulate, and divert products on the conveyor system. Sensor selection depends on product characteristics (size, shape, color, material), detection range, response speed, and environment.</p>
<h3>Photoelectric Sensors (Photoeyes)</h3>
<p>Photoeyes are the most common conveyor sensor. They detect product presence by emitting a light beam and detecting its reflection or interruption. Retroreflective photoeyes bounce light off a reflector mounted on the opposite side of the conveyor, the product breaks the beam, triggering detection. Diffuse (direct reflection) photoeyes detect light reflected from the product surface, no reflector needed, but range and reliability are lower. Through-beam sensors use a separate emitter and receiver on opposite sides of the conveyor for the highest reliability and fastest response time. Photoelectric sensors for conveyor applications are available from all major industrial sensor suppliers.</p>
<h3>Proximity Sensors</h3>
<p>Inductive proximity sensors detect metal objects without contact. On conveyors, proximity sensors verify roller rotation (using a target bolt on the roller end), detect metal pallets or containers, and monitor chain position. Capacitive proximity sensors detect non-metallic objects (plastic containers, cardboard, liquids) at short range. Proximity sensors are more resistant to dust, moisture, and contamination than photoeyes.</p>
<h3>Encoders</h3>
<p>Rotary encoders measure shaft rotation to calculate conveyor belt speed and product position. Incremental encoders generate pulses per revolution, the PLC counts pulses to determine distance traveled. Absolute encoders provide a unique position value at every shaft angle, used for precise indexing applications. Encoders mount on the drive pulley shaft, motor shaft, or a measuring wheel that contacts the belt. Product tracking systems use encoder data to follow individual products through the conveyor system for sortation and diverting.</p>
<h3>Safety Sensors</h3>
<p>Safety light curtains protect operator access points where guards are not practical. Safety area scanners monitor zones around conveyor intersections and robot cells. Safety sensors connect to the safety PLC or safety relay to implement emergency stopping when an operator enters a hazardous area. Safety sensor selection and placement must comply with <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a> and OSHA requirements. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-safety/">conveyor safety guide</a> for detailed standards and requirements.</p>
<h2 id="guards-heading">Guards &amp; Guarding</h2>
<p>Guards prevent operator contact with moving conveyor components, nip points, drive components, belt edges, and rotating shafts. <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.219" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.219</a> requires guarding of all power transmission components. ANSI/ASME B20.1 provides specific guarding requirements for conveyor systems.</p>
<p>Nip point guards cover the pinch points where the belt wraps around drive pulleys, tail pulleys, and snub pulleys. These are the most critical guard locations, nip points can pull in clothing, fingers, and hair. Guards must be fixed (bolted) and require a tool for removal. Interlocked guards connect to the safety circuit so that removing the guard stops the conveyor motor.</p>
<p>Drive guards enclose the motor, gearbox, belts, chains, and sprockets. Perforated metal or wire mesh guards allow airflow for motor cooling while preventing contact. Underguards cover the return strand of belt conveyors to prevent contact and to catch material that falls off the conveyor. Side guards prevent product from falling off the conveyor edges.</p>
