<h2 id="faq-heading">Design FAQ</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">In what order should conveyor components be specified?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Start with the product and the environment, then work outward. Define product size, weight, surface, and throughput, plus washdown, temperature, and food-contact requirements. Choose the conveying surface next, because belt or roller type sets minimum pulley diameter, drive method, and take-up style. Then set drive position and size the drive from belt pull and speed. Select the frame material and profile for the load and environment, and match bearings and fasteners to it. Finish with sensors, controls, guide rails, and guarding at each transfer and access point.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What frame material should I use for a conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Carbon steel (painted or powder-coated) is the standard choice for dry, non-food environments and is usually the lowest-cost option. Stainless steel 304 is the common choice for food-grade washdown environments. Stainless steel 316 is specified where chlorides, acids, or aggressive sanitizers are present. Aluminum extrusion frames offer lightweight, corrosion-resistant, reconfigurable construction for cleanroom, pharmaceutical, and light-duty applications. Stainless frames cost noticeably more than equivalent carbon steel frames because of material and sanitary welding and finishing.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between an end drive and a center drive conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An end drive places the motor and drive pulley at the discharge end of the conveyor. This is the most common configuration, and the carrying strand of the belt is pulled toward the drive. A center drive places the drive module underneath the conveyor, typically near the midpoint, and wraps the belt around the drive pulley on the return side. Because neither end carries the drive pulley, both ends can use small-diameter nose rollers for tight product transfers, and many center-drive designs can run in either direction.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What sensors are used on packaging conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Common sensors include photoelectric sensors (photoeyes) for product detection, retroreflective photoeyes for product presence, through-beam sensors for precise detection, proximity sensors for metal detection and roller rotation verification, rotary encoders for speed and position tracking, and ultrasonic sensors for height measurement. These connect to the PLC or zone controller for conveyor start/stop, accumulation, diverting, and alarm functions.</p>
</div>
</div>
